From: Martin Pluskal <martin@pluskal.org>
Subject: Drop the V8-based code-mode workspace members

The v8-poc, code-mode-runtime and code-mode-host workspace members depend on
the v8 crate, whose build script downloads a prebuilt librusty_v8 archive from
the network (or clones chromium) and therefore cannot be built in an offline
build environment.  They implement the "code_mode" feature, which upstream
ships as Stage::UnderDevelopment with default_enabled = false, so removing them
does not change the behaviour of the shipped codex binary.  Dropping them also
keeps ~213 MB of unbuilt V8 sources out of the vendor bundle.

The code_mode_host flag naming one of the dropped members is Stage::Stable and
on by default, but it only selects the session provider in
core/src/thread_manager.rs, and the surviving provider spawns the very
codex-code-mode-host binary this patch drops; the in-process alternative lived
in the removed code-mode-runtime.  Either way the code_mode tool is registered
only when the UnderDevelopment code_mode flag is on.

Since 0.166.0 the patch also removes the [patch.crates-io] entry pinning
v8 to a git revision: with no workspace member left using the crate, cargo
still resolves the git source at lock time, which fails offline.  Removing
the one line restores the offline build; the remaining git patches
(crossterm, tungstenite) are used and stay vendored.

The surviving code-mode-protocol member's build script executes a vendored
prebuilt protoc (protoc-bin-vendored) at build time, which cannot ship in
the sources.  The patch drops that build-dependency and takes the compiler
from the PROTOC environment variable instead, i.e. the distribution
protobuf (BuildRequires: pkgconfig(protobuf), PROTOC export in %build).

Downstream-only distribution policy, not suitable for upstream submission.

